From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] cuda/hip: fold the MoE routing-weight multiply into the mul_mat_id decode epilogue (+1.5% decode, byte-exact)

Decode census on the r13+0032+0033 build: 941 dispatches per token for 5.86 ms
of kernel inside a 7.28 ms wall token, so every node removed is worth its own
kernel time plus ~1.5 us of graph-replay overhead. 121 of those dispatches are
`k_bin_bcast`, and the single largest group is one launch per layer applying
the MoE routing weights:

    experts = ggml_mul(experts[n_embd, n_expert_used], weights[1, n_expert_used])

40 launches per token, 1.52 us each, to move 128 KB through the memory system
and apply EIGHT scalars. The matvec that produced those bytes already has the
finished value in a register and already knows which destination channel - i.e.
which expert slot, i.e. which weight - it is writing.

This patch adds `dst_scale` to the mm-fusion argument struct: one f32 per
destination channel, applied to `result` immediately before the store, with the
store redirected to the MUL's destination. One scalar load per block. The
multiplicand is the same rounded f32 the stock kernel would have written and
the MUL would have read back, so the fold is **bit-exact** - and measurably so:
server greedy output is byte-identical to the fold-off control on 6/6 prompts.

`dst_scale` is deliberately NOT routed through the existing `has_fusion`
template flag, and that distinction is the whole performance story:

- with `has_fusion=true` the kernel also instantiates the gate path, whose
  `tmp_shared_gate` doubles the block's LDS footprint. For the Q5_K expert-down
  launch (nwarps=8, one warp actually launched) that halves the LDS-limited
  occupancy: the fused matvec went 12.92 -> 14.72 us, eating most of the
  saving, and the whole patch measured **+0.76%**.
- `has_fusion` also applies `result += x_biases[j]` unconditionally, which is
  the identity on everything except -0.0, so it silently cost byte-exactness
  as well (4/6 prompts drifted).
- with a dedicated `has_dst_scale` template parameter the expert-down launch
  keeps its stock cost and its stock bits, and the same patch measures
  **+1.52%**, byte-exact.

Detection is a two-node subgraph (MUL_MAT_ID, MUL) via `ggml_can_fuse_subgraph`
plus: exactly one token (`ne[2] == 1`, the decode shape), a contiguous f32
factor of shape [1, n_expert_used] (so the index is `channel_dst`), identical
layout between the MUL's destination and the matvec's, and
`ggml_cuda_should_fuse_mul_mat_vec_q` so the node was going to take the mmvq
route anyway. Prefill (ncols_dst > 1) is untouched and measured neutral.
`GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MOE_WEIGHT_FUSE=1` restores the stock pair.

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0):

- same-binary toggle A/B, 5 interleaved rounds: tg128 137.38-137.69 off ->
  **139.47-139.73** on, median per-round ratio **1.0152**, 5/5 disjoint;
  pp512 neutral (median 4322.6 off vs 4323.4 on)
- dispatches 941 -> 901 per token; k_bin_bcast 121 -> 81; the 8-channel
  bin_bcast population is gone and the expert-down matvec keeps its 12.9 us
- whole-process stock vs cand, 5 rounds: tg128 80.69-80.89 -> 139.26-139.55
  (median ratio **1.7254**, was 1.6934 at 0033), pp512 median ratio 1.3489
- ppl 3.9271/3.9268/3.9271/3.9254/3.9286 vs stock 3.9314 (-0.07 to -0.15%);
  fold-off control same session 3.9243-3.9380
- test-backend-ops MUL_MAT_ID / MUL / MUL_MAT / SSM_CONV / GATED_DELTA_NET OK
- server greedy 6/6 BYTE-EXACT vs the fold-off control, and 6/6 A/A

---

